---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-b6`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) Reference',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamins & Minerals', item: `${SITE}/vitamins` },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Vitamin B6', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine): PMS, Morning Sickness, and Toxicity Risk"
  description="Vitamin B6 reference — RDA, deficiency signs, morning sickness dosing, PMS evidence, and the sensory neuropathy toxicity concern with high-dose supplements."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamins", label: "Vitamins & Minerals" }, { href: "/vitamin-b6", label: "Vitamin B6" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine)</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitamin B6 is a family of six related compounds; pyridoxal-5-phosphate (PLP) is the
      active coenzyme form. It participates in over 100 enzymatic reactions — protein
      metabolism, neurotransmitter synthesis (serotonin, dopamine, GABA), and haemoglobin
      formation. It has one of the narrowest safety margins among water-soluble vitamins.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Daily requirements</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li><strong>Adults 19–50:</strong> 1.3 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Men 51+:</strong> 1.7 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Women 51+:</strong> 1.5 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Pregnancy:</strong> 1.9 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Lactation:</strong> 2.0 mg/day.</li>
      <li><strong>Tolerable Upper Intake Level:</strong> 100 mg/day — above this, risk of sensory neuropathy rises with duration.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Toxicity — the sensory neuropathy risk</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Chronic supplementation at 100–200 mg/day or more for months to years can cause a
      dose-dependent sensory neuropathy — tingling, numbness, and impaired proprioception in
      hands and feet. Reports exist even below the UL. If you take a B-complex, check the B6
      dose — many products contain 25–100 mg per pill, and stacking with a multivitamin can
      easily exceed safe chronic intake. Symptoms are usually reversible if caught early.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Clinical uses with evidence</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Morning sickness (nausea of pregnancy)</strong> — B6 10–25 mg every 6–8 hours is a first-line, safe intervention (ACOG guideline). Often combined with doxylamine (Diclegis / Diclectin).</li>
      <li><strong>PMS symptoms</strong> — B6 up to 80 mg/day showed modest benefit in a Cochrane meta-analysis. Doses above 100 mg increase neuropathy risk with limited additional benefit.</li>
      <li><strong>Hyperemesis gravidarum</strong> — adjunctive to standard antiemetic therapy.</li>
      <li><strong>Carpal tunnel syndrome</strong> — traditional use lacks good randomised evidence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Deficiency</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Rare in isolation; more often part of general B-complex deficiency in alcoholism,
      malabsorption, or extended isoniazid therapy for tuberculosis (which depletes B6).
      Symptoms: microcytic anaemia, dermatitis, glossitis, depression, confusion, and
      seizures in severe cases. Isoniazid patients are routinely co-prescribed pyridoxine
      25–50 mg/day.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Food sources</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-1">
      <li>Chickpeas (1 cup cooked): 1.1 mg</li>
      <li>Beef liver (3 oz): 0.9 mg</li>
      <li>Yellowfin tuna (3 oz): 0.9 mg</li>
      <li>Salmon (3 oz): 0.6 mg</li>
      <li>Chicken breast (3 oz): 0.5 mg</li>
      <li>Potato (1 medium baked): 0.4 mg</li>
      <li>Banana (1 medium): 0.4 mg</li>
      <li>Fortified breakfast cereal: 0.5–2 mg</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Persistent tingling or numbness in hands/feet
      while on a B6-containing supplement warrants stopping the supplement and consulting a
      physician.
    </div>

    <EndOfContentAd />
    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-b6" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
